    "final": "To list failed systemd units using `systemctl --failed`, you can run the following command:\n\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed\n```\n\nThis command will output a list of all systemd units that failed to start, along with their status and error messages.\n\n### Example Output:\n```\n\u25cf bluetooth.service - Bluetooth\n   Loaded: loaded (/lib/systemd/system/bluetooth.service; enabled; vendor preset: enabled)\n   Active: failed (Result: exit-code) since Fri 2023-10-05 12:34:56 UTC; 2min ago\n   Failed: exit-code (Exit code is 127)\n   Process: 1234 ExecStart=/usr/bin/bluetoothd\n   Main PID: 1234 (bluetoothd)\n   Status: exited (Result: exit-code)\n```\n\n### Notes:\n- The `--failed` flag tells `systemctl` to only show units that are in a failed state.\n- It's useful for debugging why a service didn't start.\n- You can also use `systemctl status <unit>` to get more detailed info about a specific unit.\n\n### To see all failed units in a more readable format:\n```bash\nsystemctl --failed --no-pager\n```\nThis disables the pager (like `less`) so output is shown directly in the terminal.\n\nLet me know if you want to filter by a specific unit or check logs!",
